        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Here is a high-level summary of **current legal restrictions** relevant to AI and “AI agency” (i.e., when AI systems act or are treated as agents) in major jurisdictions. This is *not* legal advice — just an overview of what’s on the books and in process.  \n\n---\n\n### 🇺🇸 United States  \n**Federal level**  \n- There is *no* comprehensive federal law that regulates all AI systems or gives them independent legal agency. citeturn0search5turn0search4  \n- The entity[\"legislation\", \"Take It Down Act\", 0] (S.146) addresses non-consensual intimate images (including deepfakes) and requires platforms to remove specified content. citeturn0search11  \n- Executive action: A draft federal Executive Order seeks to **pre-empt** state AI regulation, indicating significant federal interest in setting national uniform policy. citeturn0search0turn0search3  \n- Many AI-related laws are *state-level*. citeturn0search4turn0search1  \n\n**State level (examples)**  \n- entity[\"legislation\", \"ELVIS Act\", 0] (Tennessee): Regulates use of AI for voice and likeness impersonation. citeturn0search17  \n- Some states prohibit AI systems from using licensed professional titles (e.g., medical) or from certain uses without human oversight. citeturn0search1  \n- Example: entity[\"legislation\", \"Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act\", 0] (Texas) sets limitations on development/deployment of AI systems; effective Jan 1 2026. citeturn0search8  \n\n**Implications for “AI agency”**  \n- Because no federal law recognises AI systems as legal persons or agents with independent liability, any “agency” of AI is still mediated by human/ corporate actors.  \n- Liability typically falls on providers, deployers or users of AI rather than the AI itself.  \n- Where laws restrict certain uses (e.g., impersonation, non-consensual imagery), they reduce the autonomy of AI systems acting without human oversight.  \n\n---\n\n### 🇪🇺 European Union  \n- The entity[\"legislation\", \"Artificial Intelligence Act\", 0] (EU AIA) has been adopted and introduces a risk-based regulatory regime for AI systems in the EU. citeturn0search16turn0academia24  \n- Key restrictions include:  \n  - Prohibition of “unacceptable risk” AI systems (e.g., certain types of biometric identification, social-scoring systems) under Article 5. citeturn0search16  \n  - Obligations for “high-risk” AI systems: conformity assessments, documentation, transparency, human oversight. citeturn0academia24  \n  - National authorities must be designated; sanctions regime up to significant fines. citeturn0search16  \n\n**Agency implications**  \n- The EU framework regulates systems depending on risk rather than granting them independent legal status.  \n- Human oversight and provider accountability are emphasised — so AI acting as “agent” still requires human/controller responsibility.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Key Themes / Restrictions  \nHere are recurring types of restrictions relevant to AI agency:  \n- **Use-case bans or strict limits**: e.g., real-time biometric identification, social scoring, impersonation without consent.  \n- **Transparency and documentation**: requiring providers to publish model details, risk assessments, safety frameworks.  \n- **Human oversight / control**: ensuring humans remain in loop especially for high-risk decisions.  \n- **Liability and accountability**: assigning responsibility to humans/entities deploying AI, since AI cannot (yet) hold legal personhood.  \n- **Sector-specific regulation**: e.g., healthcare, mental-health chatbots, employment decisions.  \n- **Pre-deployment requirements**: conformity assessments, third-party audits, regulatory sandboxes.  \n- **State versus federal tension** (in the US): many state laws are emerging; federal law is still in early­-stage.  \n\n---\n\nIf you like, I can **pull together a detailed table** listing *all* known AI/agency-related laws in the US (federal + each state) and major international jurisdictions (EU, UK, China, etc.), showing what each prohibits/permits and how they treat “agency” of AI. Would that be helpful?"
          ]
        },
